WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.830
Your UX portfolio, case study and 90-day plan.

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.250
This is the video for module 4 of UX Design Fundamentals.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:18.250
You already have the pieces of a real UX project: interviews, a persona, a flow, wireframes, a review and test results.

4
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.950
First you bring them together in one research pack, with participant codes, never names.

5
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.210
Then you add visual design to the tested structure.

6
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.180
Then you tell the story so employers, clients and community leaders understand it.

7
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.710
And finally you plan what comes next.

8
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:50.780
Add colour only after the structure has been tested. Choose one main colour, one accent, greys, red for errors and green for success. Check contrast for every text colour.

9
00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:56.830
Choose one font family with a heading size, a body size and a small size.

10
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.310
Design a button once and choose Create component in Figma. When you change the main component, every copy updates.

11
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:15.050
Save your colours and text sizes as styles, and replace placeholders with real hairstyle names and fake but realistic phone numbers.

12
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:29.380
For example, Blessing's final salon prototype has 6 screens, including a slot just taken screen and a payment failure screen. She retests with 3 customers, and all 3 complete the booking.

13
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.360
Her research pack is a 14-page PDF with a link to the prototype.

14
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:41.170
Before you submit, check these five points, and open your share link on someone else's phone to make sure it works.

15
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:53.390
Employers look for how you think, not just finished screens. Start with who struggled with what, then say what you did yourself and your limits, such as budget, devices or data.

16
00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:05.410
Share your top 2 or 3 themes with a short quote. Then show one before and after, because side-by-side comparisons backed by test numbers convince people most.

17
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.840
End with your real numbers and an honest reflection.

18
00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:14.600
You will tailor the opening for each reader, but the evidence never changes.

19
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:23.240
Blessing writes three openings. For a design agency job, she leads with her research methods and her iterations.

20
00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:30.890
For the salon owner, she leads with customers: shorter booking time, and every tester booked without help.

21
00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.380
For a women's business network, she leads with inclusion and who benefits.

22
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:48.680
Use your exact test numbers and say how many people took part. A practice project can say, concept project, tested with 8 participants. Employers respect that.

23
00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:55.390
Never show someone else's work as yours, and ask a client before showing their work publicly.

24
00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:02.120
Saying, my price layout confused four of five people, so I changed it, shows strength, not weakness.

25
00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:14.440
For example, Halima is a mother of two in Abuja with about 6 hours a week. She splits 90 days into three 30-day blocks, each with one goal and a number to check.

26
00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.740
First, a second project tested with 5 users.

27
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:27.480
Next, she offers a low-cost heuristic review to 2 local businesses each week, until one agrees and gives a written testimonial.

28
00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:41.850
Then her first paid project. Beware offers that ask you to pay for a guaranteed job. Real clients agree a written scope first, and Halima takes a deposit by bank transfer or a Paystack payment link.

29
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:50.740
Every Friday she spends 15 minutes ticking off actions. Plan for the hours you really have, not the hours you wish you had.

30
00:03:51.990 --> 00:04:05.530
To remember. Show your evidence. Polish only what has been tested. Tell a clear, honest story for each reader. To earn your certificate, complete the capstone and score at least 70 percent in the final quiz.

31
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:13.200
Your turn. Build your pack, write your case study, record a 2-minute summary, and write your 90-day plan.

32
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.490
Continue with the lessons below.
